<LearningGuideLayout
  title="How to Create Triggers in Blender for Godot"
  description="Draw trigger volumes in Blender, filter participants, and dispatch named Godot gameplay events with Folded Paper Engine."
  summary="A trigger is invisible in the finished game, but it belongs to the art of the level: exactly where the music changes, the greenhouse wakes up, or a doorway begins its little performance. Author that space where you can see it—in Blender."
  relatedSlugs={[
    "make-doors-switches-in-blender",
    "animation-frame-events-godot",
    "godot-trigger-system",
  ]}
  fpeHeading="What FPE creates in Godot"
>
  <div class="guide-summary">
    <strong>You will make:</strong> a clearly named Blender volume that reacts when
    the intended participant enters or exits and dispatches a reusable Scene Event.
  </div>
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/blender/trigger-invisible-checkboxes.png"
    alt="DoorTrigger volume selected in Blender with Trigger and Invisible enabled"
    caption="Use a simple volume for the trigger. Enable Invisible when helper geometry should not render in the game."
  />
  <h2>Shape the gameplay space in Blender</h2><ol>
    <li>
      Add a cube where the event should happen and scale it to cover the useful
      area. A doorway trigger should span the opening; a music region may cover
      a room.
    </li><li>Apply transforms so the exported size is predictable.</li><li>
      Give it a descriptive name such as <code>GreenhouseEntryTrigger</code>.
    </li><li>
      Open <strong>Item → Folded Paper Engine</strong> and check <strong
        >Trigger</strong
      >.
    </li><li>
      Also check <strong>Invisible</strong> when the cube is only a gameplay volume
      and should not be drawn in Godot. Leave it visible only when the object is
      meant to be seen as well as used as a trigger.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <h2>Choose who can activate it</h2><p>
    Open <strong>Trigger Events</strong>. Use <strong>Trigger Groups</strong> to
    limit participants instead of accepting every physics body in the scene. Pick
    a stable gameplay group used by the player or intended character. This keeps
    a rolling crate from starting dialogue or changing the level.
  </p>
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/blender/trigger-events-panel.png"
    alt="FPE Trigger Events configured for the player interaction OPEN_WORKSHOP_DOOR"
    caption="Trigger Events defines who can activate the volume, how it activates, and the stable event name Godot receives."
  />
  <h2>Dispatch meaning, not implementation</h2><ol>
    <li>
      Add a trigger event for entering or exiting, according to the problem you
      are solving.
    </li><li>
      Choose a stable Scene Event name such as <code>ENTERED_GREENHOUSE</code>, <code
        >LEFT_COTTAGE</code
      >, or <code>START_TEA_DIALOGUE</code>.
    </li><li>
      In the <strong>Scene Events</strong> panel, create the matching event and add
      the commands it should perform.
    </li><li>
      Start with one visible result—play an animation or speaker—before building
      a long command chain.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <h2>Test it like a level designer</h2><ul>
    <li>
      Walk through from every direction and check the activation boundary.
    </li><li>Test the exit as well as the entrance when both matter.</li><li>
      Decide whether repeat activation is delightful, harmless, or a bug.
    </li><li>
      Keep the trigger mesh simple. The player cannot appreciate a complicated
      invisible collider.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Fragment slot="fpe"
    ><p>
      FPE imports the Blender trigger as a tracking area with a simple collider,
      participant filtering, and named event dispatch. The Scene Event can play
      or stop animations, control speakers, switch cameras, load levels or
      sub-scenes, start conversations, pause activities, or dispatch another
      event. Because the trigger announces meaning, engineers can attach custom
      Godot behavior without moving the authored boundary out of Blender.
    </p><p>
      <a
        href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yGIMhpo312M&list=PLM1XlwxBilobr6XVs-7H6O_mMXAiHZEcz"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">Watch the Triggers tutorial</a
      > or <a href="/docs/fpe-triggers-commands.html"
        >read every trigger option and Scene Event command</a
      >.
    </p></Fragment
  >
</LearningGuideLayout>
